First note that bootstrapping does not alter the point estimates. They
only affect the standard errors (and z-values and p-values).

But some standard errors are extremely large. What is probably happening
here, is that in a few bootstrap samples, you obtain some crazy values
for those parameters. And because the standard errors are just the
empirical standard deviations of the parameters across bootstrap
samples, outliers may have a dramatic effect. You can check this by
extracting the bootstrap-based parameters:

BOOT <- lavInspect(model2A_med, "boot")

The standard errors reported in the output are simply based on

apply(BOOT, 2, sd)

You can manually remove the rows (in BOOT) that contain the outliers and
recompute them again.

Another possibility that may or may not work is to use bounds. For dRAR,
you may force the variance to be positive by replacing

dRAR ~~  dRAR

by

dRAR ~~ lower(0.001)*dRAR

A more aggressive approach could be to use bounds = "standard" to
enforce lower and upper bounds on most parameters (except regressions).

(Not sure what the con > 0 is doing in the syntax)

Yves.

